a rare sight in the rest of araucania  region some 30 indigenous maputo leaders  agreeing to meet the country's president  Gabriel boric to hear his proposals  solving the conflict in South Central  Chile  the region is under a state of emergency  patrolled by the Army and militarized  police  attacks by armed mapucha groups escalate  as they call for the return of their  ancestral lands  Al Jazeera was given exclusive access to  the meeting which at first was tense  outlined a plan to create a commission  with the participation of indigenous  leaders to determine how much land can  be returned to the mapuchis and by what  means  representing the Palencia Community  Maria Romero capil had a counter  proposal there is  the Chilean state must recognize its  crimes of genocide committed against the  mapuche people in the context of the  so-called pacification of the araucania  other countries that live in peace with  their indigenous people have had to  recognize these crimes and compensate  them in order to have peace  but there's another stumbling block to  dialogue the militarization of the  region  in an extensive interview with President  bodic I asked about his change of heart  on the issue you had said during in your  campaign that the first thing or one of  the first things you would do would be  to lift the the state of emergency and  it didn't work  yes exactly one of the things one has to  be able to do when you face certain  realities and you see that the things  you would like aren't working is to  change course we lifted the state of  emergency and we were obliged to  re-establish it in May because the  violence escalated potentially you had  once said that you thought it was  necessary to sit down with members of  these armed groups to have a dialogue  with them do you still believe that  I believe it's necessary to talk to all  those who are willing to have dialogue  you can't say I'm going to talk about  the conflict but not talk with all the  actors that are involved that would be  absurd those who are willing to talk  will be part of the debate we propose  so far the rebel groups say they're not  interested and those who are are making  demands the president bodage will  unlikely meet as long as the violence  continues  to see a Newman Al Jazeera Santiago
 

